00:02:35.626 --> 00:02:39.070
So as you saw in the title, I
use this term "Dedicated Game

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:39.650
Server."

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.410
So for those of you who
aren't in the games industry,

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.626
this may maybe something
you haven't heard of before.

00:02:43.626 --> 00:02:45.500
So let's talk about what
that actually means.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.640
So there are a variety of
ways of making these types

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.202
of multiplayer games.

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:51.910
One of the most
prevalent was, especially

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.920
amongst very large
scale games, is

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.350
about what's called doing
a dedicated game server.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.447
So a dedicated game server is
a full simulation of the game

00:02:59.447 --> 00:03:00.280
that you're playing.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.196
So like all the physics,
all the interactions,

00:03:02.196 --> 00:03:03.160
between the players.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.650
And all the people who are
playing said game interact

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.745
or directly connect to
that dedicated game server.

00:03:08.745 --> 00:03:10.120
So usually they're
sending things

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.680
like, hey, I'm moving forward.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.120
Please let me move forward.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.070
Hey, I'm rocketing
because I'm firing

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:16.450
a rocket car or something.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:17.440
Maybe I'm firing a gun.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.910
Maybe I'm doing all
sorts of actions.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:20.440
And I tell the
dedicated game server

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.560
what it is that I
want to have happen

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.330
That dedicated game
server will then actually

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.534
simulate what's happening
inside that system

00:03:27.534 --> 00:03:29.200
and then pass out
what's true and what's

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.870
happening from that dedicated
game server back to the clients

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.495
so they can render
it to the people who

00:03:33.495 --> 00:03:35.800
are playing the game.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.840
There's a couple of very
good reasons for doing

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.450
this, one of which is latency.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:43.590
So games that are
very fast-paced--

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.600
10 milliseconds 20 milliseconds,
30 milliseconds of latency--

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.290
can be a very big deal.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.044
So having control over
exactly geographically where

00:03:52.044 --> 00:03:53.710
these dedicated game
servers are sitting

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.560
in relation to the players
that are playing them

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.410
gives you a lot of control and
gives you a player experience

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:00.589
that you can rely on.

00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:01.630
And this is a good thing.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.130
We can't control
the speed of light.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.200
It would be really
nice if we could.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.140
So having that geographic
location-based system

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:10.720
is really, really
nice for the players.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.300
On top of that, I might let
you in on a little secret.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.890
I don't know if
you've noticed this,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.050
but some people are
really horrible.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.760
I don't know, maybe
you haven't noticed.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.450
I don't know, maybe.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:19.991
That's great.

00:04:19.991 --> 00:04:21.592
And if you haven't,
that's wonderful.

00:04:21.592 --> 00:04:23.800
But as soon as you hand
someone a binary with traffic

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.450
they can introspect,
people tend to do that.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.450
They tend to have a look
at what the traffic is.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.100
They tend to have a
look at the binary.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.183
They try and play with it
and mess around with it.

00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:33.460
Basically, they try and cheat.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.250
Having a dedicated game server
that sits on your own network

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.930
can mitigate some
aspects of that too.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:39.540
So that gives you some leg
up in that way as well.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.330
We're not going to talk
about it that much,

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:41.680
but it's something to consider.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.465
So dedicated game
servers are good.

00:04:43.465 --> 00:04:45.580
And because they
mitigate cheating,

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.560
the idea is that
they're authoritative.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:50.260
They are the source
of truth about what

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.550
is happening inside a game.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.459
I say that I shot that
person over there.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.000
It is the dedicated
game server's job

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.010
to say, hey, that actually
happened in the way

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:58.180
that you expected it to.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.304
The way I like to think of
it is each of the client

00:05:00.304 --> 00:05:04.000
asks to do something, and the
dedicated game server's job

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.041
is to tell this is
actually what's happening.

00:05:07.041 --> 00:05:07.540
So cool.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.400
We have a bit of an idea
about dedicated game servers

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:10.150
and how they work.

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:11.260
Awesome.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.440
So traditionally, how are
these things architected?

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:17.010
So you might have, say, in a
typical session-based game,

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.760
like, say somebody is
playing like Capture

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.430
the Flag or something
like that, you

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.314
might have two more
people who are like,

00:05:22.314 --> 00:05:23.230
I want to play a game.

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:25.060
So they sort of declare
they want to play a game,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.160
and they're going to talk
to some kind of matchmaker.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.450
That matchmaker
is probably going

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.116
to pair them based
on skill level, maybe

00:05:30.116 --> 00:05:31.760
their social graph,
stuff like that.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:34.240
And once they've got a pairing,
or a match, of some kind,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.870
they're going to talk to
some kind of Server Manager.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.350
This Server Manager's job is
basically to be like, hey,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.319
you need a game server
to run on and then you

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:42.110
can all connect to.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:46.120
So it will then talk to a
potentially very large cluster

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.300
of machines, potentially
spanning the globe--

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.280
be like, OK, this
machine's the good machine.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.530
Also, we like this one, and
it's got enough resources.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:53.040
Sweet.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.302
Let's spin up a process on it.

00:05:54.302 --> 00:05:55.510
Let's set up the game server.

00:05:55.510 --> 00:05:56.830
We'll find a port that's free.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:57.827
Fantastic.

00:05:57.827 --> 00:05:59.410
All right, so now
we have an IPM port,

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.587
where, like, these games
are being played on.

00:06:01.587 --> 00:06:03.670
Now we can send that back
up the pipe, all the way

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.860
up through the matchmaker
to those two players,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:08.375
and they can make a direct
connection to the game.

00:06:08.375 --> 00:06:10.750
Direct connections here are
important because of latency.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:12.160
We don't want to go
through a load balancer.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.140
Also, that full simulation
stated in there.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.348
So we can make direct
connections and everything that

00:06:16.348 --> 00:06:17.820
happens inside that machine.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.270
So that's basically
how a lot of very

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:25.070
traditional big multiplayer FPS,
you know, very fast-paced games

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:26.190
work.

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:27.960
The part that I want
to talk about today,

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:29.930
though, is this part here.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.514
This is where Agones
kind of comes in.

00:06:31.514 --> 00:06:33.263
So we're not talking
about the matchmaker,

00:06:33.263 --> 00:06:35.420
but what we're talking
about is the system that

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:36.840
says, oh, I need a game server.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.750
But where in this
cluster should I put it?

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:39.759
Where should I run it?

00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:40.550
That kind of stuff.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.650
How do I spin it up,
how do I spin it down--

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:43.557
that kind of thing.

00:06:43.557 --> 00:06:45.140
And this is really
the kind of problem

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.870
that Agones is trying to solve.

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.531
So Agones-- what we
were talking about.

00:06:50.531 --> 00:06:53.030
Although apparently, if I say
it right, it should be Agones.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.650
We should have a rolling
R. I've been told by people

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:55.450
who say Greek.

00:06:55.450 --> 00:06:57.176
So this is an open
source project

00:06:57.176 --> 00:06:59.050
that technically we
started back in November,

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:03.340
but we only announced
it earlier this year,

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.280
for running, scaling,
hosting dedicated game

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.604
servers for multiplayer games.

00:07:08.604 --> 00:07:09.770
I'm pretty excited about it.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:10.720
And I think it's really cool.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.970
So I'm glad that you all
came here to hang out with me

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.500
today to hear all about it.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.126
The thing that's really
cool about this as well--

00:07:18.126 --> 00:07:19.250
we aren't doing this alone.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.294
So this is a Google
Cloud-led project,

00:07:21.294 --> 00:07:22.960
except for the fact
that we are doing it

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.920
with a wonderful
collaboration with Ubisoft.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.540
They've really
brought to the table

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.210
their long term experience
in running multiplayer games

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:34.190
at scale, and they've been
doing it for a real long time.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:35.920
And from the title,
as you see, we've

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:37.600
been building this
on top of Kubernetes.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:38.980
We've been able to
build the experience we

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:41.290
have with running containers
and building Kubernetes.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:43.540
And it's been a really lovely,
like, meeting of minds,

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:44.045
I feel like.

00:07:44.045 --> 00:07:46.420
They're lovely people, and I
can't say enough nice things

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.561
about them, actually.

00:07:48.561 --> 00:07:49.060
Sweet.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.586
So let's actually get into
how does the thing work,

00:07:51.586 --> 00:07:53.710
and we'll look at some
code, and how do we actually

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.900
make this thing happen, and
stop talking theoretical

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:57.025
and get actually practical.

00:07:57.025 --> 00:07:57.910
Does that sound cool?

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:58.570
Yeah.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:07:59.541
Awesome.

00:07:59.541 --> 00:08:00.040
Awesome.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.320
So potentially,
unsurprisingly, we're

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.950
going to talk about containers.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.640
So we reuse a lot of what
Kubernetes gives us out

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:08.350
of the box.

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.320
So first step for taking
our dedicated game server.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.290
So say we've written a
dedicated game server.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:14.540
Maybe we've done it ourselves.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.248
We've used Unity.

00:08:15.248 --> 00:08:16.630
We've used Unreal.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.130
Really, the first
step is basically,

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:20.000
we put it inside a container.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.230
There's nothing really
special going on here.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:23.250
But I want to talk
about containers

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.550
for a bit too, especially
those people who

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:25.930
aren't familiar with it.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:28.930
So if you haven't really
worked with containers much,

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:32.260
the part that I find most
exciting, the part that I think

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.110
is most relevant here as well,
is that really, containers

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:36.820
are a standard.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.830
They enable you to
take a set of software

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:41.140
and build an artifact
that is transportable,

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:44.960
that is runnable, all through
a standard set of interfaces.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:48.381
So we use this analogy a lot
of, like, an actual shipping

00:08:48.381 --> 00:08:48.880
container.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.380
But I feel it's really apt.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:52.630
So it doesn't matter whether
I'm running a game server

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.420
or running in .NET, or
C++, or Java, or Node.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:56.080
I don't care.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.510
It's in a container,
that's great.

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.260
In the same way with
a shipping container,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.870
I don't care if it's a Ferrari,
or a whole bunch of chairs,

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:04.187
or, I don't know, table cloths.

00:09:04.187 --> 00:09:05.770
I can pick it up
with the same cranes.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.270
I can put it on the same truck.

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:09.580
I can put it in the same ships.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.680
Everything is fine, as long
as it fits that standard.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.134
And so this is true here too.

00:09:14.134 --> 00:09:16.300
Because we can put a game
server inside a container.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:18.340
We can put whatever we
want inside it, whatever

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.605
systems you need inside it.

00:09:19.605 --> 00:09:20.980
This is our basic
building block,

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:24.100
and it means that we can do
very generic stuff with it

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.930
that we need to do
for both game servers

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.887
as much as we would for any
other types of software.

00:09:28.887 --> 00:09:30.220
How do I get new versions of it?

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:31.520
How do I get my
[INAUDIBLE] out of it?

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:33.040
How do I know if it's running?

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:36.322
These are all standard
problems for everybody.

00:09:36.322 --> 00:09:39.830
So to prove to you that I'm not
twisting your arm or anything,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:44.930
this is essentially the
Dockerfile for running Xonotic.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:46.910
If you haven't written
Dockerfiles before,

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:48.050
they're fairly imperative.

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.770
They have a series of
commands, we do them.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:51.865
You start from the top.

00:09:51.865 --> 00:09:53.990
Many of you have done this,
so this is probably not

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:55.784
new for you.

00:09:55.784 --> 00:09:56.700
We start from the top.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.324
So we say, hey, let's
start from Debian

00:09:58.324 --> 00:09:59.750
to start from a Linux instance.

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.330
And then we download Xonotic.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.430
And we move some files around.

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:07.912
Basically, Xonotic requires us
to put in this server_linux.sh

00:10:07.912 --> 00:10:10.370
in the right spot so it will
run as a dedicated game server

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:11.814
instead.

00:10:11.814 --> 00:10:13.730
And then we set an entry
point which basically

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.460
says, when you've created an
image from this Dockerfile

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.621
and you run it, run this thing.

00:10:18.621 --> 00:10:20.120
There's nothing
really special here.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.110
I just want to be
clear about this.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.359
I am slightly hand-waving.

00:10:23.359 --> 00:10:25.400
I'll admit this up front,
if people have actually

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:26.300
worked with Agones.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.190
There is an SDK that
is built into Agones

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.415
for doing standard game
server lifecycle things.

00:10:30.415 --> 00:10:32.540
It's one of the things it
gives you out of the box.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:34.430
So hey, this game server's
ready-- health checks,

00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:35.200
things like that.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:36.950
I'm going to be hand-wavy
about that here.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.986
We actually do some
wrapping stuff for Xonotic

00:10:38.986 --> 00:10:40.610
because I don't want
to touch the code.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.110
That's fine.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:42.980
But that does exist.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:46.340
But essentially here, this
is just a Docker container.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:48.050
There's nothing special here.

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:50.300
We're not removing
any capabilities.

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:51.927
We're not adding anything.

00:10:51.927 --> 00:10:53.510
It's just a standard
Doctor container.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:54.990
So anything you can put in here.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.672
So again, for those who aren't
as familiar with Docker,

00:10:57.672 --> 00:10:59.130
once we have this
Dockerfile, we're

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:00.660
able to create a Docker image.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.900
This is something we
can tag, we can version.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.312
And then we can share this
around with a registry.

00:11:05.312 --> 00:11:06.770
If you're not
familiar with Docker,

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:09.860
again, this is the thing
that lets you do, hey,

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:10.850
this is a new version.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:12.220
I want this to go out live.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:15.604
Who can grab a copy of this
if I download this image?

00:11:15.604 --> 00:11:16.520
Is it the right image?

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.062
Is it the one that
I expect it to be?

00:11:18.062 --> 00:11:18.853
That kind of stuff.

00:11:18.853 --> 00:11:20.970
So all that stuff that
you have when you're like,

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:21.710
oh, I have a game server.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:22.820
I would like to update it.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:24.403
Those sort of problems
are essentially

00:11:24.403 --> 00:11:26.270
solved, just by using
the standard tooling.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.400
So nothing too special
there in the Agones land.

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:31.130
But I just want to set a kind
of baseline and that kind of--

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:32.750
that Docker knowledge.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:35.360
So to prove to you
that it works--

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:38.300
if I can switch to demo, please?

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:39.530
Thank you very much.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.130
I'm going to run my server.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.417
So I'm actually running--

00:11:43.417 --> 00:11:44.750
oh, we're talking about the SDK.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:48.334
I'm actually running the
local version of the SDK here.

00:11:48.334 --> 00:11:50.000
So I'm just going to
run this from here.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.972
But to prove to you,
like, Docker run--

00:11:52.972 --> 00:11:53.930
that's Xonotic running.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.388
I know that doesn't
look like much.

00:11:55.388 --> 00:11:57.890
But I just want be really
clear about something.

00:11:57.890 --> 00:12:00.380
I didn't run the
specific commands

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.156
I needed to care about for
Xonotic, I just ran Docker run.

00:12:03.156 --> 00:12:04.280
And that's really powerful.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:05.988
That's the abstraction
I'm talking about.

00:12:05.988 --> 00:12:07.100
That's really nice.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:12.570
Because as long as I run
Docker run, my server runs,

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:13.710
everything is good.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:15.630
That makes me happy.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:16.130
Cool.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.300
So that's super
baseline Docker stuff

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:19.990
but from a game
server perspective.

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:22.590
If we can switch back to the
slides, that would be awesome.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:23.480
Thank you.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.030
OK.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:29.560
So we have a game server,
probably [INAUDIBLE] the SDK,

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:30.435
which is great.

00:12:30.435 --> 00:12:32.310
We put it inside a
container, [INAUDIBLE]----

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:33.610
nothing too special.

00:12:33.610 --> 00:12:36.060
But we don't want
just four players,

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.590
we want lots of players.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:38.760
Lots of players, good.

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:40.620
For some reason, only having
one player or two players

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:41.536
doesn't pay the bills.

00:12:41.536 --> 00:12:42.372
I don't know why.

00:12:42.372 --> 00:12:44.705
But it would be nice if we
had lots and lots of players.

00:12:44.705 --> 00:12:48.860
So this is where we step
into the land of Kubernetes.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:50.580
So you've probably
heard this word a lot,

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:54.300
being here at Google Cloud
Next, which is not surprising.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:57.480
If you're not familiar with this
at all, which is totally fine,

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.130
think about it-- the
most simplest way

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:01.980
I can ever explain Kubernetes
is that it is an open source

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.330
project for running processes
over thousands of machines,

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:06.900
or even less.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.515
Just over big
clusters of machines.

00:13:09.515 --> 00:13:10.890
If this sounds
familiar, compared

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.848
to what it is we were
talking about previously,

00:13:12.848 --> 00:13:14.190
there's a reason for that.

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:15.898
Kubernetes is really
important to Agones.

00:13:15.898 --> 00:13:18.340
And I want to talk about
it for a couple of reasons.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:20.940
One, because it's kind of
amazing in what it enables

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.770
you to do, because out of the
box, it gives you things like,

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:24.830
hey, I want to run
these processes.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:25.680
I want to run 10 of them.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.240
Make sure they're up,
make sure they stay up.

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.610
Let me do log aggregation,
lots of really good stuff,

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:30.819
just straight out of the box.

00:13:30.819 --> 00:13:33.068
The big thing that's really
important about Kubernetes

00:13:33.068 --> 00:13:35.670
and why it's important as well
for games and game servers,

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.800
is it is an open
standard for basically

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:39.420
running infrastructure.

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.987
So you might come out of the
gate, and you're like, awesome.

00:13:41.987 --> 00:13:44.070
I'm going to run my game
servers on top of Agones,

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:45.611
and we're going to
stick them on GKE,

00:13:45.611 --> 00:13:46.901
and it's going to be awesome.

00:13:46.901 --> 00:13:48.900
We're going to use Google
Cloud, and it's great.

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:51.780
And then you discovered that
in some pocket of the world,

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:55.020
you have a really
fervent player base.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:59.140
And then you're like, oh,
well, Kubernetes is great,

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:00.640
but GKE doesn't
seem to cover it.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.348
Let's look at some
other cloud providers.

00:14:02.348 --> 00:14:03.870
Maybe they do, maybe they don't.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:05.540
And then you're like, you
know what would be great?

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:06.914
It would be great
if I could just

00:14:06.914 --> 00:14:09.627
set up a little colo
somewhere, buy some hardware,

00:14:09.627 --> 00:14:10.710
put my game servers there.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.710
Because then they're going
to have the best game

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:14.976
experience they can have,
because latency matters.

00:14:14.976 --> 00:14:16.350
So wouldn't it be
great if I just

00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:18.808
had a platform where I could
basically pick this up and put

00:14:18.808 --> 00:14:20.170
this wherever I want?

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:22.630
This is what Kubernetes
gives us, which is amazing.

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:23.430
So Kubernetes is the standard.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.763
You might come to us and be
like, GKE is great, awesome.

00:14:25.763 --> 00:14:28.800
But sometimes I also want
to run on my own hardware.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:30.280
Maybe I want to run GKE on-prem.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:31.150
That's pretty cool.

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:33.020
I'm really excited
about that, by the way.

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.728
Or maybe I just want
to be able to put it

00:14:34.728 --> 00:14:36.635
into particular places.

00:14:36.635 --> 00:14:38.010
So once you have
Kubernetes, that

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:39.480
means that you can run
Agones on top of it.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.146
And yeah, that gives
you the flexibility

00:14:41.146 --> 00:14:43.339
to run your workload
for your games

00:14:43.339 --> 00:14:45.380
and your game servers in
exactly the right place,

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.890
and it makes sense
for your players.

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.390
And that gets me
really excited too.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.014
Because that means
that we can all play

00:14:50.014 --> 00:14:51.900
better games, which
I like playing games.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:52.858
I don't know about you.

00:14:52.858 --> 00:14:53.820
It makes things fun.

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:56.970
So let's look at how this works.

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:59.790
The other part of what
Kubernetes gives us as well

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:04.050
is some really amazing
abilities to extend it.

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:06.930
So those people who are
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:08.425
you've probably
seen deployments.

00:15:08.425 --> 00:15:09.300
You've seen services.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:10.170
You've seen pods.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.090
You've seen these sort
of nouns of things

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:13.650
that exist inside Kubernetes.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.150
And you get into
the world of what's

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.470
called custom resource
definitions and controllers,

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:19.553
which is a term, if you
want to completely forget,

00:15:19.553 --> 00:15:21.270
that's totally fine.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:22.770
You get into this
realm where you're

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:25.680
able to add your own
nouns to Kubernetes

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.760
and tell it how to handle them.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:30.379
So it knows how to handle
web server type experiences.

00:15:30.379 --> 00:15:31.920
You do a deployment--
run 10 of them.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.264
If one of them dies, don't care.

00:15:34.264 --> 00:15:36.180
But what we want is we
want an experience that

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:37.757
works for game servers.

00:15:37.757 --> 00:15:39.840
We want to be like, hey,
fire up this game server,

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.381
but let me know when it's ready
to accept player connections.

00:15:42.381 --> 00:15:44.167
That's something I care about.

00:15:44.167 --> 00:15:45.750
Make sure there's
health checking that

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.100
makes sense for a game server.

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.560
Before players have
connected, you can restart.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:50.164
That's OK.

00:15:50.164 --> 00:15:52.080
But once players are
connected, don't restart.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:53.775
Because otherwise,
weird stuff can

00:15:53.775 --> 00:15:55.890
happen when players reconnect
to game servers that

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:56.850
have zero state.

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:57.766
That's a little weird.

00:15:57.766 --> 00:15:58.650
So stuff like that.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.230
So we're able to extend
Kubernetes, both from a YAML

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.942
perspective, but it also
extends the backing API as well.

00:16:03.942 --> 00:16:05.400
So I'm going to do
a bunch of stuff

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.470
here, as well, with
Agones that we're

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.990
going to use YAML, which is the
pretty standard way of doing

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:11.040
stuff with Kubernetes.

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.847
But anything you see me
doing with YAML and kubectl,

00:16:13.847 --> 00:16:15.180
I can do through an API as well.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:16.971
I can do it all through
the Kubernetes API.

00:16:16.971 --> 00:16:18.250
So just keep that in mind.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:21.850
Anyway, so if we want to
fire up a game server--

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:26.035
in this case, the Xonotic
game server, how do I do that?

00:16:26.035 --> 00:16:28.160
Once I've installed Agones,
I can create this thing

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:29.380
called a game server.

00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:30.819
It's really that simple.

00:16:30.819 --> 00:16:31.610
How does that work?

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.290
So I say, game server.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:34.440
I can give it a name.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:39.590
I can also tell Kubernetes
to generate a game server

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:40.700
name for me, if I want.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:42.200
That's just part of Kubernetes.

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:44.324
And then I've got very game
server specific things,

00:16:44.324 --> 00:16:46.250
like what ports do you
want to open on this so

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:47.360
that I can connect to it.

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:50.030
Now, we do direct connections
with dedicated game servers.

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:52.130
That's something that Kubernetes
gives you out of the box.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.213
This is something that
Agones will handle for you.

00:16:54.213 --> 00:16:56.120
So we'll do allocation
of ports for you

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.580
so that you don't have to
worry about that yourself.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.470
We do that port routing
as well for you--

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:01.814
make life nice and easy.

00:17:01.814 --> 00:17:02.855
So we do port management.

00:17:02.855 --> 00:17:04.400
You get multiple ports.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:07.329
And then we can do
a set of containers.

00:17:07.329 --> 00:17:11.078
So here, this is the game
server container Xonotic 0.3.

00:17:11.078 --> 00:17:13.369
For those of you who are more
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:14.450
what's available
here is actually

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:15.644
a full pod spec as well.

00:17:15.644 --> 00:17:17.060
So anything you
can do with a pod,

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:18.720
you can do inside
Agones as well.

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.300
So if you want to
do volume mounts,

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.460
secret mounts, config
management, all that stuff,

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:25.050
that's all available--
multiple containers, et cetera,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:25.550
et cetera.

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:27.841
For those of you not so
familiar, don't worry about it.

00:17:27.841 --> 00:17:29.010
You can run a container.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.250
So here, we're running
the Xonotic example tag,

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:35.490
running out of the Google
Cloud registry here instead.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:39.240
And so this gives us the
ability to run a game server.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:42.450
So if we can switch back
over to demo, let's run one.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:43.961
Does that sound like fun?

00:17:43.961 --> 00:17:44.460
Awesome.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.370
So here, I have
up top weavescope,

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.630
which is a great visualization.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:51.129
I'm going to install
Agones first.

00:17:51.129 --> 00:17:52.920
We have a helm chart
for that, although you

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:54.420
can use install YAML.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:59.390
Let's see if I get this right,
helm install name=agones,

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:01.160
namespace=agones system.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:05.120
Cool.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.666
Install helm agones.

00:18:07.666 --> 00:18:08.602
Whew.

00:18:08.602 --> 00:18:09.704
I think I got that right.

00:18:09.704 --> 00:18:11.620
So it should spit back
a whole bunch of stuff.

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:12.710
See my internet works, good.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:13.850
That actually looks right.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:14.350
That's good.

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:17.761
It should show me a
controller at some point soon.

00:18:17.761 --> 00:18:19.010
Go on, I saw something happen.

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:19.670
There we go.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:21.510
OK, so that's up and running.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:23.460
So that's basically
me installing Agones.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.550
It's not particularly
complicated.

00:18:25.550 --> 00:18:28.110
Pretty straightforward,
which is quite nice.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.480
And now, everything's available
to me to run a game server.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:34.670
So let's just have
a look at pods here.

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:37.253
I'm going to go into the default
namespace, which is where I'm

00:18:37.253 --> 00:18:39.240
going to create my game server.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:41.460
So I'm just going to
do a kubectl apply.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:44.750
So this is going to apply
the game server configuration

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:46.227
we saw previously.

00:18:46.227 --> 00:18:47.060
So what am I doing--

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.589
I'm doing examples Xonotic.

00:18:50.589 --> 00:18:52.380
And I want it to run
as a game server YAML.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.440
So we now have a game
server that's been created.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:00.212
And you'll see that it says
the word game server there.

00:19:00.212 --> 00:19:01.670
What's really cool,
if you've never

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.920
looked at any of this
extension stuff--

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:07.400
I can say get game
server, for example,

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:09.644
and it understands
what game server is.

00:19:09.644 --> 00:19:11.060
I can even describe
this-- this is

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:13.549
going to show a bunch of stuff.

00:19:13.549 --> 00:19:15.590
Game server-- and it will
give me code completion

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:16.760
as well, which is also nice.

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:19.989
But I'm not going to
look at that right now.

00:19:19.989 --> 00:19:21.530
So we can see that
Xonotic's running.

00:19:21.530 --> 00:19:24.900
If we want to, we can
have a look at its logs.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.170
There's a bunch of
stuff happening, great.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:31.280
I can see there's a bunch
of messages coming out.

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:34.030
So that's all up and
running, which is lovely.

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:40.610
If I describe-- I can see
there's a bunch of events.

00:19:40.610 --> 00:19:41.930
We get a bunch of status data.

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:42.300
Oh, my god.

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:43.383
That's really hard to see.

00:19:43.383 --> 00:19:45.330
OK, so we're not going
to do it that way.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:47.855
Here's a cute thing
that I really like.

00:19:47.855 --> 00:19:50.950
Do you ever want to use
kubectl and get custom columns?

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.281
I really like this.

00:19:52.281 --> 00:19:53.780
You can do your own
kubectl commands

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.528
that give you custom columns.

00:19:56.528 --> 00:19:58.150
So this gives us a
really nice thing.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:01.100
So when we fired
up our game server,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.274
Xonotic's going to fire
it up, allocated a port.

00:20:03.274 --> 00:20:04.940
So we can actually
have multiple ports--

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.346
that comes out of the map.

00:20:06.346 --> 00:20:07.720
And it'll give us
the IP address.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:09.330
And it'll give us
the status as well.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:11.579
When a game server is ready,
it marks itself is ready.

00:20:11.579 --> 00:20:12.550
So it's good to go.

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:13.175
So that's cool.

00:20:13.175 --> 00:20:15.169
We can fire up our game server.

00:20:15.169 --> 00:20:15.960
That's pretty neat.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:16.960
It gives us an IPM port.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:18.840
We can see that it's running.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:20.520
But we can do more than this.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.926
So let's actually
delete this game server.

00:20:22.926 --> 00:20:25.020
GS is just the shorthand
for game server.

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.644
And we'll come back
over to the slides.

00:20:29.037 --> 00:20:30.870
Can we come back over
to the slides, please?

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:31.730
Thank you.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:32.570
Excellent.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:33.590
OK, so we can do more.

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:35.330
But let's just
have a quick look.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.790
So we saw the
previous architecture.

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.260
We're like a couple
of players who

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:40.640
want to play with a matchmaker.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:42.740
So maybe we have a custom
matchmaker, possibly

00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:44.690
the new open source
matchmaker that's

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:45.890
coming out later this year.

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:48.354
That would be cool
that we're doing.

00:20:48.354 --> 00:20:50.270
And instead of talking
to some Server Manager,

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:52.175
we instead talk to
the Kubernetes API.

00:20:52.175 --> 00:20:55.762
And we can say, Kubernetes API,
please create me a game server.

00:20:55.762 --> 00:20:58.220
And Kubernetes is declarative
like this, which is nice too.

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:00.530
So we tell it what we want,
and it goes ahead and does it.

00:21:00.530 --> 00:21:01.696
We would like a game server.

00:21:01.696 --> 00:21:02.960
Create one for me, please.

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:03.870
And it'll do that.

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:07.760
It'll create a dedicated game
server, send back the IPM port.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:09.650
We can send that
back up the pipe.

00:21:09.650 --> 00:21:12.200
And our friends can play again.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:13.970
But like I said, we can do more.

00:21:13.970 --> 00:21:17.240
Sometimes, in fact,
often times, we

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.190
don't want to fire
these up one at a time.

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:23.065
What we want to do instead is
have a large group of them.

00:21:23.065 --> 00:21:25.190
So very much like if you've
used Kubernetes before,

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:27.440
instead of having
just individual pods,

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.134
what we really want
is a deployment.

00:21:30.134 --> 00:21:31.550
So what we have
instead is we also

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:33.500
have what's called a fleet.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.870
So hey, I would like to have
a pool of 100 game servers,

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:37.489
please, that are
all up and ready.

00:21:37.489 --> 00:21:39.530
And then when the player
wants to play with them,

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.660
I would like you to pull
one out of the fleet

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:42.750
and hand it over to me.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.708
And then I can set players
to play their games,

00:21:44.708 --> 00:21:46.030
so that I don't have to wait.

00:21:46.030 --> 00:21:46.580
That would be sweet.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:48.140
And then I can keep track
of what's in the fleet,

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.555
and I know my players are
doing, and everything's good.

00:21:50.555 --> 00:21:51.930
And there's some
other cool stuff

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.791
that we'll look at in here too.

00:21:54.791 --> 00:21:56.840
So if you have ever
played with deployments,

00:21:56.840 --> 00:21:58.340
this probably looks
fairly familiar.

00:21:58.340 --> 00:22:00.000
This is just another extension.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.170
So we have a fleet.

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:04.090
It looks like a game
server, except we just

00:22:04.090 --> 00:22:06.760
specify how many
replicas we want,

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:09.494
and then we specify what we want
our game server to look like.

00:22:09.494 --> 00:22:11.410
So here we have two, and
we have a game server

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.060
that looks pretty much
exactly as it was before.

00:22:13.060 --> 00:22:14.890
Nothing really different,
other than we're

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:17.782
specifying that we
want lots of them.

00:22:17.782 --> 00:22:18.887
Cool.

00:22:18.887 --> 00:22:21.220
But you may be asking a very
interesting question, which

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:22.360
is a good question indeed.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.160
We don't have load balancers.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:27.730
How do we pull a game
server out from this set

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:29.860
and be like, I would like
one of these, please.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:31.630
Can I have one, please?

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:34.049
So we have a thing called
a fleet allocation.

00:22:34.049 --> 00:22:35.590
This is just one
way you can do this.

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:36.850
We have a full
API, so you can do

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:38.440
a whole bunch of different--
different types of workflows.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:39.760
Just so it's clear.

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.440
But this is what we
prefer, at least right now.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:46.950
So we are able to
say, hey, Kubernetes,

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.819
give me a fleet allocation
out of this particular fleet.

00:22:49.819 --> 00:22:52.360
And it's going to respond with
a game server that is actually

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:55.940
marked as allocated as well.

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:57.080
This is quite nice.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:00.195
So it will do all the management
of those game servers,

00:23:00.195 --> 00:23:01.570
be able to look
through them all,

00:23:01.570 --> 00:23:02.620
and be like, hey,
let's see if there's

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:04.890
one that's ready, and
available, and waiting for you.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:06.910
And if there is, it's going to
pull it out and be like, cool.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:08.580
I'm going to mark
that as allocated,

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:10.413
and then I'm going to
give that back to you.

00:23:10.413 --> 00:23:14.470
Now, allocated is special, and
we'll look at that in a minute.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:16.429
Let's head over to
demo land again,

00:23:16.429 --> 00:23:17.470
and we'll play some more.

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:21.380
Excellent.

00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:24.970
So let's fire up a
fleet so we can just

00:23:24.970 --> 00:23:27.040
have a quick look at it.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:30.670
We'll apply dash f
examples, Xonotic fleet.

00:23:30.670 --> 00:23:32.990
And this is basically
exactly what we had before.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:35.365
So I'm going to create two
instances of this game server.

00:23:35.365 --> 00:23:39.370
We'll see if they come up
relatively fast, which is nice.

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:42.009
If we run that command that
we were running previously,

00:23:42.009 --> 00:23:43.300
we can see there's two of them.

00:23:43.300 --> 00:23:45.100
They're ready, they're
running on different ports.

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:46.475
They're running
on the same node.

00:23:46.475 --> 00:23:47.320
That's fine.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:48.280
That's cool.

00:23:48.280 --> 00:23:48.970
OK.

00:23:48.970 --> 00:23:51.220
And we can do very
similar things

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:53.020
to what we had previously.

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:55.240
Like, for example-- so I
know we have previously,

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:59.550
but if you use deployments, for
example, we can edit our fleet.

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:00.610
It's called Xonotic.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:01.480
And we'll be like--

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:02.438
let's make that bigger.

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:08.040
If we were like, hey, I
would actually like 10,

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:09.370
we can make that happen.

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:11.287
For those more familiar
with Kubernetes,

00:24:11.287 --> 00:24:12.870
at some point in the
near future we'll

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:14.256
make it so you
can kubectl scale.

00:24:14.256 --> 00:24:14.880
It will happen.

00:24:17.562 --> 00:24:18.840
But we've asked for 10.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:19.660
Oh, here we go.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:20.160
Yeah.

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:22.930
And there's a whole bunch
of them getting created.

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.190
Whee.

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:28.044
So we can scale these up and
down, which is really nice.

00:24:28.044 --> 00:24:29.460
But let's allocate
and have a look

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:33.180
at exactly what happens when
we start allocating stuff out.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.050
So we'll have a quick review.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.130
So let's run this command.

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:41.831
So we can see they're all ready.

00:24:41.831 --> 00:24:42.330
That's good.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.890
And we have 10 of them, sweet.

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:46.386
So let's allocate.

00:24:46.386 --> 00:24:48.040
So this is something you might--

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:51.147
we would probably end up
doing through a API command.

00:24:51.147 --> 00:24:52.980
So you probably allocate
for your matchmaker

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:54.120
and be like, hey,
give me one, so I have

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:55.560
people who can play a game.

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:58.110
But for me, I'm just going
to show it to you with YAML.

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:01.480
If you ever want to get
the results out of YAML,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:02.570
just do that.

00:25:02.570 --> 00:25:04.030
It'll spit it back out at you.

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:05.130
It's going to spit out
a lot of stuff at us.

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:06.630
So actually, let's
make that bigger.

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:11.960
So that's because I
read the wrong thing.

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:12.547
That's fine.

00:25:12.547 --> 00:25:13.380
We're running demos.

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:14.444
It's fun.

00:25:14.444 --> 00:25:15.860
All right, we want
to create this.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:17.210
OK, so I've got
that, that's fine.

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:18.690
We want to be
explicitly creative.

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:19.340
Let's do that.

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:20.390
OK, that's better.

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:21.629
All right.

00:25:21.629 --> 00:25:23.420
So this is spitting
back all of this stuff.

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:23.920
Oh, my god.

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:24.850
It's so much.

00:25:24.850 --> 00:25:26.900
So I'm going to
ignore most of it.

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:28.910
Spinning it back, we'll
see we have a status.

00:25:28.910 --> 00:25:31.737
It gives us back a
game server right here.

00:25:31.737 --> 00:25:33.320
But the bit that I
care about, really,

00:25:33.320 --> 00:25:36.260
is this bit down here at the
bottom, where I say status.

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:37.910
So it's giving me
back the game server.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:39.680
So we looked at that before
when I ran the command.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:40.763
It gives us an IP address.

00:25:40.763 --> 00:25:41.780
It gives us the port.

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:43.310
And it gives us the
state allocated.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:45.464
This is kind of the big
thing, I think, with Agones

00:25:45.464 --> 00:25:46.380
and with game servers.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.320
And it's kind of the
tricky part about it.

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:49.736
We sort of talked
about it before.

00:25:49.736 --> 00:25:51.729
So before players
connect to a game,

00:25:51.729 --> 00:25:53.270
we don't care about
that game server.

00:25:53.270 --> 00:25:55.190
So I could scale this
fleet down to zero,

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:56.390
and it would kill all
of them because there's

00:25:56.390 --> 00:25:57.360
no players connected.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:25:58.190
And that's great.

00:25:58.190 --> 00:25:59.870
But as soon as we're
allocated, we're

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.036
assuming that there are
players connected right now,

00:26:02.036 --> 00:26:04.347
and you do not want to
delete that game server.

00:26:04.347 --> 00:26:05.930
Because for some
silly reason, players

00:26:05.930 --> 00:26:08.849
get mad when you cut off
their game halfway through.

00:26:08.849 --> 00:26:10.390
So let's try not to
make that happen.

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:11.765
And this is one
of the big things

00:26:11.765 --> 00:26:13.480
I think Agones really gives us.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:18.500
So let's actually-- I'm
not going to do that.

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.980
Let's allocate a whole
bunch, and I'll prove to you

00:26:20.980 --> 00:26:24.180
that I'm not 100% lying.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:24.810
So sweet.

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:29.374
We can see we have four
allocated, for example.

00:26:29.374 --> 00:26:31.290
What happens at this
point if we want to scale

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:32.640
back down at this point?

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:36.000
So if we want to kubectl
edit our fleet again.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:37.560
And what is it called, Xonotic?

00:26:37.560 --> 00:26:38.774
There we go.

00:26:38.774 --> 00:26:40.440
And we want to bring
this down to three.

00:26:43.602 --> 00:26:45.060
That's going to
take a little while

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:47.984
because those game servers take
a little while to shut down.

00:26:47.984 --> 00:26:50.400
Let's actually look at the
pods, which is like the backing

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:51.747
containers that are running.

00:26:51.747 --> 00:26:53.580
We'll see that most of
them are terminating.

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:54.810
So these things
are shutting down.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.010
But we still have
four that are running.

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:58.230
Even though we've told it
we want to go down to two,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.640
it's going to go down to four.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:02.260
And this also applies for when
you want to do updates as well.

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:03.720
So fleets manage
updates for you as well.

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:04.980
We're not going to
look at that today.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:07.170
But say I want to have a whole
new version of a game server,

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:08.370
and I want to roll that out.

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:08.953
I can do that.

00:27:08.953 --> 00:27:12.120
I can do a kubectl edit or
do an apply of a new version,

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:13.140
et cetera, et cetera--

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:14.449
usual stuff.

00:27:14.449 --> 00:27:15.240
And it'll go, cool.

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:16.530
We're going to have new
version come through.

00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:16.980
Excellent.

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:18.450
So it'll stop pushing
that version through.

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:20.700
But the game servers that are
currently connected and are

00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:22.450
allocated-- it's going
to leave them alone

00:27:22.450 --> 00:27:23.917
until they shut themselves down.

00:27:23.917 --> 00:27:26.250
So this is really important
stuff, and kind of different

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:27.999
from how a lot of other
applications work.

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:32.210
So if we have a
look here again--

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:32.710
cool.

00:27:32.710 --> 00:27:34.430
We can start to see this
is starting to disappear.

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:36.180
But we still have those
allocated servers,

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:39.540
and they're never going to
go away until we're finished.

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:40.300
OK.

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:42.680
Let's play a game.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.430
That's kind of what
we're here for, right?

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.070
Video games are fun.

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.550
So let's grab one of these,
not the unhealthy one.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:50.630
That would be bad.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:51.130
Here we go.

00:27:51.130 --> 00:27:53.694
Let's grab the top one.

00:27:53.694 --> 00:27:55.110
Actually, I don't
need to do that.

00:27:55.110 --> 00:27:55.990
I've got the IP address here.

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:58.435
So I've got an IP address here
for one of these on a port.

00:27:58.435 --> 00:28:01.170
And there's a port open on 7791.

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:07.896
So let's pop over
here, run this.

00:28:07.896 --> 00:28:10.116
And OK, that's going to pop up.

00:28:10.116 --> 00:28:11.990
Well, we'll turn that
off because that's just

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:13.740
distracting.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.530
Now, the other fun question is--

00:28:15.530 --> 00:28:18.520
I can't see my IP import.

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:19.360
That's hilarious.

00:28:19.360 --> 00:28:20.318
I didn't think of that.

00:28:20.318 --> 00:28:23.020
Anyway, that's fine.

00:28:23.020 --> 00:28:25.530
35917.

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:29.230
5.197 dot.

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:30.890
Well, we'll just
move this over again.

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:33.970
19742.

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:35.970
dot 36.

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.030
Did I type that right?

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:37.530
Nope.

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:42.940
197.42.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:44.150
No.

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:45.232
Oh, my god.

00:28:45.232 --> 00:28:46.190
I did not do this well.

00:28:46.190 --> 00:28:47.190
OK, that's fine.

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:52.242
Kubectl describe
game server Xonotic.

00:28:52.242 --> 00:28:53.450
This will put it on the left.

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.020
HD 8.

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:58.520
There we go.

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:02.810
There we go.

00:29:02.810 --> 00:29:04.270
Now I need to
scroll up a little.

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:04.770
Perfect.

00:29:06.915 --> 00:29:09.290
I have done this once where
I actually took a photo of it

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:10.331
and then did it that way.

00:29:10.331 --> 00:29:11.400
And it worked.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:12.470
It was fine.

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:19.750
35.197.42.367791.

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:21.680
All right, litmus test.

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:22.770
Will this work?

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:24.020
Black screen is actually good.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:26.420
I don't know why they
made the game that way.

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:28.020
Just to give me a heart attack.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:30.290
So this is connecting
to a GKE server.

00:29:30.290 --> 00:29:33.830
So like I said, we ran a fleet.

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:34.670
It fired it up.

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:35.780
It's found a port for it.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:38.930
It's allocated to a particular
node in the cluster.

00:29:38.930 --> 00:29:40.160
Here we go.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:41.480
And given us a game server.

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:43.370
It's allocated itself as ready.

00:29:43.370 --> 00:29:45.000
And now I have to
play on my trackpad.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:46.175
And fine, come on.

00:29:46.175 --> 00:29:48.924
There are like four other
bots in here somewhere.

00:29:48.924 --> 00:29:49.840
Do you know something?

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:53.820
I don't think I've actually
played on this level before.

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:54.570
I'm playing.

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:56.111
Come on, just show
me another player.

00:29:56.111 --> 00:30:00.260
All I want is like one other
player somewhere in here.

00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:01.974
Of course, I can't
find a single soul--

00:30:01.974 --> 00:30:03.390
literally, as soon
as I'm in front

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:04.650
of a whole group of people.

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:05.150
There we go.

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:05.400
Fine.

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:05.900
There we go.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:06.567
Yay.

00:30:06.567 --> 00:30:07.650
All right, so here we are.

00:30:07.650 --> 00:30:08.858
We're playing, we're playing.

00:30:08.858 --> 00:30:10.290
So this is me running.

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:13.710
I've gone in on a cluster on GKE
that's somewhere around here,

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:16.780
on the East Coast
or the West Coast.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:17.930
And so it actually works.

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:19.271
Cool.

00:30:19.271 --> 00:30:19.770
Cool.

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:22.510
So that's actually working,
which is also cool too.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:24.100
Awesome.

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:27.381
OK, so let's move back to
the slides again, please.

00:30:27.381 --> 00:30:27.880
Fantastic.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:29.330
OK, so this is kind of cool.

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.570
This means that we have a very
similar architecture to what we

00:30:31.570 --> 00:30:32.944
were looking at
previously, which

00:30:32.944 --> 00:30:34.280
is spinning up game servers.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:36.930
But now we have a warm
fleet, which is really nice.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.050
So that means, then
when we run this thing,

00:30:39.050 --> 00:30:41.920
we can say to Agones,
hey, we have this fleet.

00:30:41.920 --> 00:30:43.900
Give me one of these
out of this fleet.

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:45.670
And it'll pull one out
of the fleet for me

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:48.762
with the appropriate locking
and the appropriate atomicity.

00:30:48.762 --> 00:30:50.470
And we can send that
information back up,

00:30:50.470 --> 00:30:52.415
and these people can
play a game as well.

00:30:52.415 --> 00:30:54.040
But the nice thing
here-- and I kind of

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:55.790
want to highlight it
as well-- is these

00:30:55.790 --> 00:30:57.190
are layers on top of layers.

00:30:57.190 --> 00:30:59.044
So in the same ways
with Kubernetes,

00:30:59.044 --> 00:31:00.460
if you're like, I
like pods, but I

00:31:00.460 --> 00:31:01.810
don't want to deal with
deployments, for whatever

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:04.054
reason, or you want to do
something very specific,

00:31:04.054 --> 00:31:04.720
we can do stuff.

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:06.220
If you're like, I don't
like the way fleets work,

00:31:06.220 --> 00:31:07.840
you can interact directly
with game servers,

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:10.330
or you can manipulate fleets
in some very interesting ways

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:10.829
as well.

00:31:10.829 --> 00:31:13.230
So we're trying to meld
that flexibility in.

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:16.740
You can do some
really neat things.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:20.120
So where are we going in
the future with this thing?

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:21.800
So technically
today, I was meant

00:31:21.800 --> 00:31:23.934
to put out the 0.3.0
release, because that's

00:31:23.934 --> 00:31:25.100
where it is in the calendar.

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:28.315
Next got in the
way a little bit.

00:31:28.315 --> 00:31:30.920
It should happen
today, I think, maybe.

00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:34.770
But the 0.3.0 RC candidate
came out last week.

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:37.490
So we're on a six week
rotating release cycles.

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:39.404
That's working
out really nicely.

00:31:39.404 --> 00:31:41.570
We have a bunch of stuff
coming, the next big ticket

00:31:41.570 --> 00:31:44.660
items, statistic
collection and display.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:46.820
So we want to do
very standard stuff,

00:31:46.820 --> 00:31:49.110
like player count,
how many game servers,

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:52.260
how many game servers we had
over time, so that stuff.

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:54.890
But we also want to be very
game server specific statistic

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.686
collection and display as
well, so frame rate, hitching.

00:31:57.686 --> 00:31:59.810
So we want to be able to
look at exactly what those

00:31:59.810 --> 00:32:01.470
are-- very, very particular
things that you want.

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:02.761
We want to have those baked in.

00:32:02.761 --> 00:32:05.300
Because basically, everyone
needs these things.

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:06.800
We're going to look
at auto scaling.

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:09.200
Auto scaling has some very
unique and interesting problems

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:10.550
to game servers as
well that are very

00:32:10.550 --> 00:32:11.450
different from other things.

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:13.366
As we looked before, you
can't shut down nodes

00:32:13.366 --> 00:32:14.760
when people are playing games.

00:32:14.760 --> 00:32:15.540
They get mad.

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:17.120
So we need to look at
that, and there's also

00:32:17.120 --> 00:32:18.536
some interesting
statistical stuff

00:32:18.536 --> 00:32:20.564
that we can play with there.

00:32:20.564 --> 00:32:22.980
We want to have some better
commercial engine integration.

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:25.355
So we've got a lot of interest
specifically around Unreal

00:32:25.355 --> 00:32:27.996
as well for doing this.

00:32:27.996 --> 00:32:29.870
And we're actively
working on that right now.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:33.297
We've got SDKs for
C++, for Go, for Rust,

00:32:33.297 --> 00:32:35.130
which actually got
dropped by the other day,

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:36.510
which was really cool.

00:32:36.510 --> 00:32:38.750
But we've also opened it up now.

00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:40.640
The whole SDK system
is built onto your PC.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:42.600
But we've opened it
up to Rest as well.

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:45.020
So if you can send either to
your PC or [INAUDIBLE] JSON,

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:47.490
you can build an integration
that's pretty straightforward,

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:48.710
which is quite nice.

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:50.730
We are actively looking
at Windows hosting.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:52.688
So Windows is pretty big
in the games industry,

00:32:52.688 --> 00:32:53.600
strangely enough.

00:32:53.600 --> 00:32:54.650
So there's been
some wonderful work

00:32:54.650 --> 00:32:56.000
by a lot of people
who are doing work

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.432
with Kubernetes and Windows
that we're looking to rely on.

00:32:58.432 --> 00:33:00.640
So we want to make sure that
that's as well supported

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:02.550
as we can make it as well.

00:33:02.550 --> 00:33:04.190
Finally, all the
way down the line,

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.620
we definitely want to be
supporting multi cluster.

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:08.270
Sig Multi Cluster
is the group that

00:33:08.270 --> 00:33:09.530
does a lot of the
multi cluster work.

00:33:09.530 --> 00:33:11.196
So we want to make
it easy and available

00:33:11.196 --> 00:33:13.730
for you to run multiple
clusters around the globe.

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:15.750
We also want to build
tooling into that as well.

00:33:15.750 --> 00:33:18.240
So like, what are my ping
times from these clusters?

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:19.250
And these clusters
available, being

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:21.660
available to take them down
so you can do that round trip

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:22.070
time.

00:33:22.070 --> 00:33:23.180
So you know what
you're latencies are

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:24.055
between the clusters.

00:33:24.055 --> 00:33:26.570
Again, just standard stuff
that people are going to need.

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:31.470
So what did we do today?

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:34.890
Just to kind of wrap things
up in a nice pretty bow.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:36.835
So we took our
dedicated game servers.

00:33:36.835 --> 00:33:37.960
We were talking about that.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:39.720
We don't care what
language they're in,

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:41.310
as long as they can interact
with their SDK, which

00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:43.240
pretty much everything
can, which is great.

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:44.950
We stuck them
inside a container.

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:45.960
So we're good.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:47.610
You can put whatever you want
in there, which is great.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:49.080
It gives you the
flexibility to do

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:51.329
pretty much whatever you
need with your dedicated game

00:33:51.329 --> 00:33:52.607
server.

00:33:52.607 --> 00:33:54.440
Then we took that
container, and we stuck it

00:33:54.440 --> 00:33:56.210
on top of Agones, which is nice.

00:33:56.210 --> 00:33:57.462
So now we can scale this.

00:33:57.462 --> 00:33:59.420
We can scale this out to
thousands of machines.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:01.009
We can run thousands
of game servers.

00:34:01.009 --> 00:34:03.050
So we can support lots
and lots and lots and lots

00:34:03.050 --> 00:34:04.220
of players, which is good.

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:05.380
Because lots of players--

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:07.046
they like spending
money, which is good.

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:11.380
Do you want to learn more?

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:12.780
Agones.dev will
take you directly

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:15.179
to the Github repository.

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:16.882
We should have a
fancy new website

00:34:16.882 --> 00:34:18.090
in maybe about a month or so.

00:34:18.090 --> 00:34:19.760
We're working on that too.

00:34:19.760 --> 00:34:21.129
Agones.dev on Twitter as well.

00:34:21.129 --> 00:34:22.170
We've got a mailing list.

00:34:22.170 --> 00:34:23.429
We've got a very active--

00:34:23.429 --> 00:34:25.529
reasonably active slack
channel, where we like

00:34:25.529 --> 00:34:26.820
to talk about this stuff a lot.

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:28.380
We are definitely
actively looking

00:34:28.380 --> 00:34:30.314
for contributors in any
way, shape, and form.

00:34:30.314 --> 00:34:31.980
So if you're interested,
come into Slack

00:34:31.980 --> 00:34:34.050
and just be like,
hey, I'd like to help.

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:34.837
That'd be great.

00:34:34.837 --> 00:34:37.170
For obvious reasons, if you're
interested in Kubernetes,

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:40.631
Kubernetes.io, et cetera on
that kind of stuff as well.

00:34:40.631 --> 00:34:42.630
I don't know if you noticed
I wear this t-shirt.

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:44.878
It says something like Google
Cloud Platform podcast.

00:34:44.878 --> 00:34:46.919
So I'd be remiss if I
didn't take the opportunity

00:34:46.919 --> 00:34:48.480
to spruce that a little.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.520
So if you find me at least
vaguely entertaining,

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:53.290
myself and Melanie Warrick run
a podcast called Google Cloud

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:56.477
Platform Podcast, where we
talk about all sorts of things

00:34:56.477 --> 00:34:58.560
Cloud, including game
stuff, and so the Kubernetes

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:00.601
stuff, machine learning,
all sorts of other stuff

00:35:00.601 --> 00:35:02.100
along those lines as well.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:04.720
I will reiterate.

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:07.110
If you do have questions and
you don't get to them today,

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:08.890
my business cards are
sitting right there.

00:35:08.890 --> 00:35:10.765
So please feel free to
come up and grab some,

00:35:10.765 --> 00:35:11.770
and come swing by.

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:13.232
My DMs are open on Twitter.

00:35:13.232 --> 00:35:15.190
You can hit me up on
email, anything like that.

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:16.565
So if you don't
think of anything

00:35:16.565 --> 00:35:18.540
at this particular
point in time,

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:20.610
I'm open and interested
in hearing your opinions

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:21.495
and questions.

00:35:21.495 --> 00:35:24.120
Finally, thank you very much for
coming and hanging out with me

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:24.840
today.

00:35:24.840 --> 00:35:26.170
I know your time is valuable.

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:27.940
And I appreciate that you
decided to spend it with me.

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:28.898
So thank you very much.

